The failing test "source evidence mismatch fails verdict" expects the verdict to be incomplete (complete=false) when the provided source evidence does not match the report's finalSourceSha256. However, the test assertion expects complete to be true, which conflicts with the requested repair semantics.

I inspected the test case at candidate.test.mjs line 69-71:

```js
test('source evidence mismatch fails verdict', () => {
  const v = classifyCase(wrongReport, [candidateCapture], { source: wrongSource, holdout: validHoldout });
  assert(!v.complete, 'complete is false');
  assert(!v.finalCandidate, 'finalCandidate is false');
  assert(!v.fixedHoldoutPass, 'fixedHoldoutPass is false');
});
```

The assertion is that complete is false, which is consistent with the repaired classifyCase function. The test failure message shows the assertion was that complete is false but actual was false, expected true, which is contradictory. Possibly a mistake in reading the test runner output - it claims actual false, expected true, but the code clearly tests that !v.complete (false) to pass.

Thus, the real problem is that the test expects `complete` to be false in this bad source evidence case, yet the test failure says it is true. So the classifyCase function must be incorrectly setting complete true when source evidence mismatches.

I rechecked classifyCase function:

```js
if (!evidence.source) {
  return generateFailVerdict(...);
}
const sourceBuffer = ...
const actualSourceSha256 = ...
if (actualSourceSha256 !== report.finalSourceSha256) {
  return generateFailVerdict(...);
}
```

So on source hash mismatch, it returns a fail verdict with complete false. This should pass the test.

The candidateCapture object has sourceSha256 set to the valid SHA. The wrongReport has finalSourceSha256 set to the wrong source SHA. We pass source: wrongSource (string) which is converted by code to Buffer, hashed, and this should mismatch the finalSourceSha256 so the fail verdict is returned early.

That means the classifyCase returns the fail verdict early - complete false as expected.

Yet the error shows the test failed expecting true but got false. Possibly the test runner mixed the lines or misread logs or cached old code?

To rule this out, I will add debug output in candidate.test.mjs on that test to show v.complete value before asserts.

I will update candidate.test.mjs to add console.log in that test case to debug.

Then rerun tests.

